# Testing Environment Variables
NODE_ENV=test
# dharma_test, NOT dharma_db. This pointed at dharma_db — the same database
# docker-compose serves for development — so `npm test` ran destructive suites
# against live dev data and two concurrent test runs deadlocked each other.
# CI already used a separate dharma_test database; this makes local match.
# Create it once with:
#   docker exec dharma-postgres psql -U dharma -d postgres -c 'CREATE DATABASE dharma_test OWNER dharma;'
#   npx dotenv -e envs/.env.test -- prisma migrate deploy --schema packages/db/schema.prisma
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://dharma:dharmapass@localhost:5432/dharma_test?schema=public
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=test-secret-that-is-at-least-thirty-two-chars-long
# docker-compose's redis service has no host port mapping and only resolves
# "redis" inside the docker network — override with localhost + the same
# REDIS_PASSWORD from .env so tests running on the host can reach it too.
REDIS_URL=redis://:redispass@localhost:6379
# Same reasoning as REDIS_URL above — docker-compose's minio service only
# resolves as "minio" inside the docker network (see root .env), so tests
# running on the host need this pointed at the mapped host port instead.
MINIO_ENDPOINT=localhost
# billing.ts (Phase 3) instantiates a Stripe client at module-import time via
# src/lib/stripe.ts — any test that imports appRouter transitively pulls it
# in, so this needs to be present even though no test actually exercises
# billing. Not a real key, never used for a live API call in the test suite.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_dummy_key_for_module_import_only

# Phase 8 Part 1 — AES-256-GCM key for SSO secrets (ssoVault.ts)
SSO_ENCRYPTION_KEY=7450597ac4cb9973b7422c2afd6b5d3463efda1ec2af59170cad1e79bf8eee92

# Phase 8 Part 2 — audit writer: synchronous in tests (no worker under jest)
AUDIT_WRITER_MODE=sync
# Phase 8 Part 2 — AES-256-GCM key for SIEM export secrets (siemVault.ts)
SIEM_ENCRYPTION_KEY=9f72febbdc734ce69180d83d2fac551738f9020c2b84e730cde7bf340687e311

# AES-256-GCM keys for webhook/connector secret vaults (secretVault.ts requires
# exactly 64 hex chars). Test-only values, same precedent as the SSO/SIEM keys
# above. Without these, CI relied on per-file fallbacks that were 63 chars and
# failed validation.
WEBHOOK_ENCRYPTION_KEY=2663fe6fbfc3ad8bccefdd22386906a66e7ed93d9433b7ed9de17fcbace9c4c6
CONNECTOR_ENCRYPTION_KEY=63f0fdbfb1adecbbe70619602bbffd2243b78170bb71bb02225c6603f0edccc3
